 "scan_is_truncated": "Each dataset was scanned to at most 400,000 rows, so percentages describe the scanned prefix. go2_joint_off3000000 holds 5.7M rows and 401,288 were scanned. Per-dataset 'truncated' says which.",
 "headline": "EVERY excitation dataset is 0.000% -- the collector's divergence filter works. 13 of 32 other datasets contain diverged rows, worst go2_tilt_rear at 16.8%. This asymmetry is a property of the COLLECTORS, not of the physics, and it is recorded in no dataset.",
 "W0_is_only_partly_protected": "W0 (w0_poincare_decidability.py) applies no magnitude filter. It drops an episode only when episode_events returns None, i.e. no detectable stance events. Tested directly on go2_joint_off3000000: of 150 episodes scanned, 35 (23%) contain at least one diverged row -- far above the 0.67% ROW rate. Of 8 diverged episodes, W0 would drop 6 and KEEP 2; of 8 clean episodes it drops 0. So divergence usually destroys stance detection, but not always, and roughly a quarter of diverged episodes reach the fit. R^2 is not robust to outliers. THIS DOES NOT SHOW THE W0 FIGURES ARE WRONG -- it shows they rest on data whose divergence status was never checked, and the invocation glob is not recorded in any doc, so which dataset they used cannot be recovered from the repo. Anyone who knows the glob can settle it by re-running with a magnitude filter and comparing."
}